---
// Ask Tally: how to connect an AI assistant to Tally's read-only MCP
// connector at /mcp (worker/mcp.ts). Static, no script.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/editorial.css';

const URL_MCP = 'https://tally.milux.co.uk/mcp';
const SECTIONS = [
  { id: 'address', label: 'The connector address' },
  { id: 'claude', label: 'Add it in Claude' },
  { id: 'other', label: 'Add it in other MCP clients' },
  { id: 'tools', label: 'What your assistant can do' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'What it uses and what it keeps' },
];
---
<Base title="Ask Tally from your AI assistant: Tally" description="Connect Claude or another AI assistant to Tally, the open directory of UK defence capability, and ask it questions in your own words.">
  <div class="ask-tally">
    <header class="page-hero">
      <p class="mono kicker">Connector <span class="kicker-sep">Read-only</span> <span class="kicker-sep">No sign-up</span></p>
      <h1>Ask Tally from your AI assistant<span class="stop">.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">Tally has a connector that any AI assistant supporting the Model Context Protocol (MCP) can use. Add it once, then ask your assistant things like "who does counter-drone work within 25 miles of Portsmouth?" and it will look the answer up in Tally, with links back to each company on the map.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="docs">
      <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="mono">On this page</p>
        <ol>
          {SECTIONS.map((s, i) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}><span class="mono">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>{s.label}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="docs-body">
        <section id="address" aria-labelledby="url-heading">
          <h2 id="url-heading">The connector address</h2>
          <p>Use this address wherever your assistant asks for a remote MCP server URL:</p>
          <p class="code-block code-block-lg"><span class="mono">Remote MCP server URL</span><code class="connect-url">{URL_MCP}</code></p>
          <p>There is nothing to sign up for and no key. It is free to use.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="claude" aria-labelledby="claude-heading">
          <h2 id="claude-heading">Add it in Claude</h2>
          <ol class="steps steps-list">
            <li><span class="mono step-number">01</span><p>In Claude on the web or the desktop app, open <strong>Customize</strong>, then <strong>Connectors</strong>.</p></li>
            <li><span class="mono step-number">02</span><p>Choose <strong>+ Add</strong>, then <strong>Add custom connector</strong>.</p></li>
            <li><span class="mono step-number">03</span><p>Paste <code>{URL_MCP}</code> and choose <strong>Continue</strong>.</p></li>
            <li><span class="mono step-number">04</span><p>For <strong>Authentication</strong>, choose <strong>No sign in</strong>, then choose <strong>Add</strong>.</p></li>
            <li><span class="mono step-number">05</span><p>In a chat, choose <strong>+</strong>, then <strong>Connectors</strong>, and turn Tally on. Then ask your question.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <h3>For Claude Team and Enterprise owners</h3>
          <p>An owner can add Tally for everyone in the organisation: open <strong>Organization settings</strong>, then <strong>Connectors</strong>, choose <strong>Add</strong>, then <strong>Custom</strong>, then <strong>Web</strong>, and paste the address above. Members then turn it on in a chat as above.</p>
          <h3>Claude Code</h3>
          <p>Run this command:</p>
          <p class="code-block"><span class="mono">Terminal</span><code>claude mcp add --transport http tally {URL_MCP}</code></p>
        </section>

        <section id="other" aria-labelledby="other-heading">
          <h2 id="other-heading">Add it in other MCP clients</h2>
          <p>Any client that supports remote MCP servers over Streamable HTTP can use Tally. Add a server with the address above, the HTTP (Streamable HTTP) transport and no authentication. Look for a setting called connectors, tools, integrations or MCP servers.</p>
          <dl class="spec">
            <div><dt class="mono">Address</dt><dd><code>{URL_MCP}</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">Transport</dt><dd>HTTP (Streamable HTTP)</dd></div>
            <div><dt class="mono">Authentication</dt><dd>None</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="tools" aria-labelledby="tools-heading">
          <h2 id="tools-heading">What your assistant can do</h2>
          <p>The connector is read-only. It offers four tools:</p>
          <dl class="tools card">
            <div class="card-row">
              <dt><code>search_companies</code></dt>
              <dd>Find companies by capability or capability group, region, size, accreditation, framework, membership, regional cluster or keyword, and near a UK postcode or a point within a radius. Returns up to 25 companies at a time with a summary, capabilities, location and a link to the company on the Tally map.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="card-row">
              <dt><code>get_company</code></dt>
              <dd>The public profile Tally holds for one company.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="card-row">
              <dt><code>list_capabilities</code></dt>
              <dd>Tally's grouped capability taxonomy and the accepted values for the other filters.</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="card-row">
              <dt><code>list_clusters_and_events</code></dt>
              <dd>The UK's regional defence and security clusters and their upcoming events. Members-only events are shown only as a date and a link to join the cluster.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="data" aria-labelledby="data-heading">
          <h2 id="data-heading">What it uses and what it keeps</h2>
          <ul class="points">
            <li>Answers come only from the same public data the Tally map shows. Your assistant's own model does the thinking; Tally runs no AI model for the connector.</li>
            <li>Tally does not log or keep what your assistant asks. To apply a fair-use limit, it counts requests per network address (for IPv6, per /64 network). Each count covers one minute, is held only in memory, and is never stored. Everyone sharing one network address shares one allowance.</li>
            <li>Postcodes are placed from Tally's own data. If Tally cannot place one, your assistant can give a latitude and longitude instead.</li>
            <li>The limit is generous for normal use. If you hit it, wait a minute and try again.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Something wrong or missing? Please <a href="https://milux.co.uk/contact/">get in touch</a>.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
